{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqbc3urlxyq","workspace":"/Users/imyu/Desktop/zonoid","title":"Two bench levels needed: gate unit test versus product end to end bench","summary":"Strategy (2026-06-12, user-driven). The held-out bench (bench-heldout.js) is a GATE UNIT TEST: one isolated task, one RAG inject/abstain decision, no graph, no DAG, no wiring. The PRODUCT is the whole context-assembly pipeline: a task graph where tasks are wired, DAG-tier context (a task inherits its context_deps summaries via explicit edges — always injected, no gate) PLUS RAG-tier (gated semantic) PLUS the cross-task KB flywheel. So the held-out bench measures one slice (the gate), not the product. We need a SECOND bench: product END-TO-END, comparing ON (full orchestrator: graph + DAG + RAG + gate + workers) vs OFF (plain agent, no graph), on context-REQUIRED interdependent tasks. The PUBLIC bench (SWE-bench-style real tasks + objective outcomes) is the right SOURCE for the e2e level — realism + external credibility (can't be accused of rigging your own tasks) — BUT single-issue public tasks exercise RAG/gate, not DAG; to test DAG you need the multi-task analog of a KB-dependent candidate: task B that FAILS without task A's context-edge summary and SUCCEEDS with it. STRATEGIC TIE-IN: this is exactly why ON-vs-OFF has shown PARITY so far (orchestrator-token-cost-benchmark memory: 'no benefit measured yet') — the bench tasks were self-solvable, didn't REQUIRE the process, so the orchestrator had nothing to add. The product's value only shows on tasks that NEED the whole pipeline (RAG-required: off fails without a KB note; DAG-required: off fails without a dependency's context). Two levels answer different questions: gate-unit calibrates the gate + λ; e2e proves the product is worth using (the competitive claim). Relates to [[note-mqbbndrgd1k]] (KB churn) and [[note-mqbbfla22pq]] (smoke found 0 KB-dependent).","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-12T19:43:34.017Z","vec":[-0.0588089525